<worksheet xml:id="lt12-practice-quiz">
<title>Learning Target 12: Quadratic Patterns</title>
<objectives>
<ul>
<li>I can recognize and identify key characteristics of a quadratic pattern represented in a table, graph, equation or application.</li>
<li>I can translate between different representations.</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-pattern-rectangle">
<title>Pattern 1: Growing Rectangle</title>
<introduction>
<p>Case 1 is a rectangle that is 4 blocks wide and 2 blocks tall. Each new case grows by one block in both the width and the height.</p>
<image width="95%">
<shortdescription>Four cases of a growing rectangle of blocks: 4 by 2, 5 by 3, 6 by 4, and 7 by 5.</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="lt12-rectangle-pattern">
<diagram dimensions="(660,190)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-2.5,30,6)">
<repeat parameter="case=1..4">
<repeat parameter="col=0..case+2">
<repeat parameter="row=0..case">
<rectangle center="((case-1)*(case/2+5)+col, row)" dimensions="(0.85,0.85)" fill="steelblue" stroke="black"/>
</repeat>
</repeat>
<label anchor="((case-1)*(case/2+5)+(case+2)/2, -1.6)" alignment="center">
<m>\text{Case } ${case}</m>
</label>
</repeat>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is quadratic. The block counts are
<m>8, 15, 24, 35</m>
. The differences between consecutive cases are
<m>7, 9, 11</m>
, which are not constant, so the pattern is not linear. The ratios are
<m>15/8 = 1.875</m>
and
<m>24/15 = 1.6</m>
, which are not constant either, so it is not exponential. The second differences are
<m>2, 2</m>
, and a constant second difference is the signature of a quadratic pattern.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for the number of blocks
<m>y</m>
in case
<m>n</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Case
<m>n</m>
is
<m>n+3</m>
blocks wide and
<m>n+1</m>
blocks tall, so
<m>y = (n+3)(n+1)</m>
. Expanding gives
<m>y = n^2 + 4n + 3</m>
. Check case
<m>3</m>
:
<m>9 + 12 + 3 = 24</m>
blocks, which matches the picture.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-pattern-staircase">
<title>Pattern 2: Staircase</title>
<introduction>
<p>Each case adds one more column to the staircase, and each column is one block taller than the one to its left.</p>
<image width="80%">
<shortdescription>Four cases of a staircase of blocks containing 1, 3, 6, and 10 blocks.</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="lt12-staircase-pattern">
<diagram dimensions="(560,200)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-2.5,17,5.5)">
<repeat parameter="case=1..4">
<repeat parameter="col=0..case-1">
<repeat parameter="row=0..col">
<rectangle center="((case-1)*(case/2+2)+col, row)" dimensions="(0.85,0.85)" fill="mediumpurple" stroke="black"/>
</repeat>
</repeat>
<label anchor="((case-1)*(case/2+2)+(case-1)/2, -1.6)" alignment="center">
<m>\text{Case } ${case}</m>
</label>
</repeat>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is quadratic. The counts are
<m>1, 3, 6, 10</m>
. The first differences are
<m>2, 3, 4</m>
, so the pattern is not linear. The ratios
<m>3, 2, 5/3</m>
are not constant, so it is not exponential. The second differences are
<m>1, 1</m>
, which is constant, so the pattern is quadratic.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="2in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for the number of blocks
<m>y</m>
in case
<m>n</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Case
<m>n</m>
is made of columns of height
<m>1, 2, 3, \dots, n</m>
, so
<m>y = 1 + 2 + \cdots + n</m>
. Two copies of the staircase fit together into an
<m>n</m>
by
<m>n+1</m>
rectangle, so
<m>y = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}</m>
, which expands to
<m>y = \frac{1}{2}n^2 + \frac{1}{2}n</m>
. Check case
<m>4</m>
:
<m>\frac{4 \cdot 5}{2} = 10</m>
blocks.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-pattern-two-rows">
<title>Pattern 3: Two Rows</title>
<introduction>
<p>Each case has a top row and a longer bottom row. Both rows grow by one block from case to case.</p>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>Four cases of a two-row block pattern containing 4, 6, 8, and 10 blocks.</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="lt12-two-rows-pattern">
<diagram dimensions="(640,160)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-2.5,25,4)">
<repeat parameter="case=1..4">
<repeat parameter="col=0..case+1">
<rectangle center="((case-1)*case/2+4*(case-1)+col, 0)" dimensions="(0.85,0.85)" fill="darkseagreen" stroke="black"/>
</repeat>
<repeat parameter="col=0..case-1">
<rectangle center="((case-1)*case/2+4*(case-1)+col, 1)" dimensions="(0.85,0.85)" fill="darkseagreen" stroke="black"/>
</repeat>
<label anchor="((case-1)*case/2+4*(case-1)+(case+1)/2, -1.6)" alignment="center">
<m>\text{Case } ${case}</m>
</label>
</repeat>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is linear. The counts are
<m>4, 6, 8, 10</m>
, and the pattern gains exactly
<m>2</m>
blocks from each case to the next. A constant first difference means the pattern is linear, and the second differences are all
<m>0</m>
, so it is not quadratic.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for the number of blocks
<m>y</m>
in case
<m>n</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The rate of change is
<m>2</m>
blocks per case. Working backward, case
<m>0</m>
would have
<m>2</m>
blocks, so
<m>y = 2n + 2</m>
. Check case
<m>3</m>
:
<m>2(3) + 2 = 8</m>
blocks.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-pattern-doubling">
<title>Pattern 4: Doubling Block</title>
<introduction>
<p>Each case is two blocks tall, and the width doubles from one case to the next.</p>
<image width="85%">
<shortdescription>Four cases of a two-row block pattern containing 2, 4, 8, and 16 blocks.</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="lt12-doubling-pattern">
<diagram dimensions="(600,170)" margins="5">
<coordinates bbox="(-1,-2.5,22,4)">
<repeat parameter="case=1..4">
<repeat parameter="col=0..2**(case-1)-1">
<repeat parameter="row=0..1">
<rectangle center="(2**(case-1)-1+2*(case-1)+col, row)" dimensions="(0.85,0.85)" fill="sandybrown" stroke="black"/>
</repeat>
</repeat>
<label anchor="(2**(case-1)-1+2*(case-1)+(2**(case-1)-1)/2, -1.6)" alignment="center">
<m>\text{Case } ${case}</m>
</label>
</repeat>
</coordinates>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is exponential. The counts are
<m>2, 4, 8, 16</m>
. The first differences are
<m>2, 4, 8</m>
and the second differences are
<m>2, 4</m>
, so it is neither linear nor quadratic. Each case is
<m>2</m>
times the one before it, and a constant ratio is the signature of an exponential pattern.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for the number of blocks
<m>y</m>
in case
<m>n</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The growth factor is
<m>2</m>
. Working backward, case
<m>0</m>
would have
<m>1</m>
block, so
<m>y = 1 \cdot 2^n = 2^n</m>
. Check case
<m>4</m>
:
<m>2^4 = 16</m>
blocks.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-table-a">
<title>Table A</title>
<introduction>
<p>Consider the values in the table below.</p>
<tabular>
<row bottom="medium">
<cell>
<m>x</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>0</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>1</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>2</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>
<m>y</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>7</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>12</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>19</m>
</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is quadratic. The first differences are
<m>1, 3, 5, 7</m>
, which are not constant, so the table is not linear. The ratios
<m>4/3</m>
and
<m>7/4</m>
are not constant, so it is not exponential. The second differences are
<m>2, 2, 2</m>
, so the table is quadratic.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.75in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for
<m>y</m>
in terms of
<m>x</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
For a quadratic
<m>y = ax^2 + bx + c</m>
, the constant second difference equals
<m>2a</m>
. Here the second difference is
<m>2</m>
, so
<m>a = 1</m>
. The value at
<m>x=0</m>
gives
<m>c = 3</m>
. Using
<m>x=1</m>
:
<m>1 + b + 3 = 4</m>
, so
<m>b = 0</m>
. The formula is
<m>y = x^2 + 3</m>
. Check
<m>x=4</m>
:
<m>16 + 3 = 19</m>
.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lt12-table-b">
<title>Table B</title>
<introduction>
<p>Consider the values in the table below.</p>
<tabular>
<row bottom="medium">
<cell>
<m>x</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>0</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>1</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>2</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>
<m>y</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>1</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>6</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>15</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>28</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>45</m>
</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is quadratic. The first differences are
<m>5, 9, 13, 17</m>
, so the table is not linear. The ratios
<m>6</m>
,
<m>2.5</m>
, and
<m>28/15</m>
are shrinking, so it is not exponential. The second differences are
<m>4, 4, 4</m>
, which is constant, so the table is quadratic.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="2in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for
<m>y</m>
in terms of
<m>x</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The second difference is
<m>4</m>
, and the second difference equals
<m>2a</m>
, so
<m>a = 2</m>
. The value at
<m>x = 0</m>
gives
<m>c = 1</m>
. Using
<m>x = 1</m>
:
<m>2(1)^2 + b(1) + 1 = 6</m>
, so
<m>b = 3</m>
. The formula is
<m>y = 2x^2 + 3x + 1</m>
. Check
<m>x = 3</m>
:
<m>18 + 9 + 1 = 28</m>
.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise xml:id="lt12-table-c">
<title>Table C</title>
<introduction>
<p>Consider the values in the table below.</p>
<tabular>
<row bottom="medium">
<cell>
<m>x</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>0</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>1</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>2</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>
<m>y</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>5</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>15</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>45</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>135</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>405</m>
</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is exponential. The first differences are
<m>10, 30, 90, 270</m>
and the second differences are
<m>20, 60, 180</m>
, so the table is neither linear nor quadratic. Every ratio of consecutive outputs is
<m>3</m>
, and a constant ratio means the table is exponential.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.5in">
<statement>
<p>
Find a formula for
<m>y</m>
in terms of
<m>x</m>
.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The growth factor is
<m>3</m>
and the value at
<m>x = 0</m>
is
<m>5</m>
, so
<m>y = 5(3)^x</m>
. Check
<m>x = 4</m>
:
<m>5(81) = 405</m>
.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="lt12-table-d">
<title>Table D</title>
<introduction>
<p>Consider the values in the table below.</p>
<tabular>
<row bottom="medium">
<cell>
<m>x</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>1</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>2</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>5</m>
</cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell>
<m>y</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>12</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>6</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>4</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>3</m>
</cell>
<cell>
<m>2.4</m>
</cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</introduction>
<task workspace="2in">
<statement>
<p>What type of pattern is this: linear, exponential, quadratic, or none of these? Explain how you know.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
It is none of these. The first differences are
<m>-6, -2, -1, -0.6</m>
, so the table is not linear. The second differences are
<m>4, 1, 0.4</m>
, so it is not quadratic. The ratios are
<m>0.5</m>
,
<m>0.\overline{6}</m>
,
<m>0.75</m>
, and
<m>0.8</m>
, which are not constant, so it is not exponential either. The outputs are decreasing toward zero but never at a constant rate or a constant factor.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
<task workspace="1.25in">
<statement>
<p>Since this table is not one of our three types, describe in words what is happening to the outputs as the inputs increase.</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
The outputs drop quickly at first and then level off, getting closer and closer to zero without reaching it. Each output is
<m>12</m>
divided by the input, so doubling the input cuts the output in half. This kind of relationship is called inversely proportional.
</p>
</answer>
</task>
</exercise>
</page>

<conclusion>
<p>Write a short summary you could use as a checklist: what do you look at in a table to decide whether a pattern is linear, quadratic, or exponential? Be specific about differences and ratios.</p>
</conclusion>
</worksheet>